          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Our nomadic brewery was born far from home, in a small Stockholm kitchen in 2017. I was working in Sweden when a Swedish colleague who brewed beers for our company parties opened a new world to me, handing me books and sharing his own hard-earned tricks.</p><p>With my wife by my side from the very start, we armed ourselves with BIAB, a couple of pots and a mesh bag, turning our apartment into a tiny brewhouse. We learned to mash, boil and ferment one experiment at a time—Stockholm's soft, clean water forgiving many of our mistakes. Every batch deepened our passion for homebrewing.</p><p>Some years later we moved back to Greece, to the place where we grew up, and we knew we didn’t want to leave brewing behind. We invested in proper gear – Grainfather, FermZilla and all the toys that turn a kitchen hobby into something a bit more serious – and kept pushing my recipes.</p><p>That's when a good friend joined us, brewing side by side, challenging ideas and perfectly complementing our efforts through countless failures and a few that made us think: “This deserves to be shared.”</p><p>The cost of a full brick-and-mortar brewery was completely out of reach, so we chose the path that fit our story: a nomadic brewery. We brew on other brewery’s systems, but the recipes, the process and the obsession with fresh, characterful beer are ours. Our dream is simple: one day to see our beers boiling in our own kettles, under our own logo – still with the same homebrewer <strong>soul</strong> that started in that tiny Swedish kitchen.</p>"
          }
        },
